* Inability to understand and perform the pain assessments during the study;
* Hypersensitivity or contraindication to the use of ingredients of the study drugs;
* Grade III obesity (BMI above 40);
* Presence of one or more flu-like symptoms, such as: fever, cough, dyspnea, myalgia and fatigue, respiratory symptoms, gastrointestinal symptoms (such as diarrhea) within 14 days prior to the screening or randomization visit;
* Other serious comorbidities at the investigators' discretion (such as history of renal, hepatic, cardiac or other peptic ulcer);
* Any clinical condition that the investigator considers to pose a risk to the patient or interfere with study conduction.

Inclusion Criteria:

Participants must meet all of the following criteria:

1. Women aged 18 to 50 years.
2. Histologically confirmed cervical intraepithelial neoplasia grade 3 (CIN3/HSIL).
3. Colposcopy demonstrates a complete transformation zone and the upper margin of the cervical lesion is visible (transformation zone type 1 or 2).
4. Positive HPV test at baseline.
5. Willing and able to provide written informed consent. These criteria are directly specified in the study protocol.

Exclusion Criteria:

Participants meeting any of the following criteria will be excluded:

1. Known or suspected hypersensitivity to hexaminolevulinate (HAL), related compounds such as aminolevulinic acid (ALA), or any component of the investigational formulation; or a history of porphyria, solar urticaria, or other severe photosensitivity disorders.
2. Cervical lesion involving more than 50% of the cervix.
3. Acute or active gynecologic infection.
4. Current use of an intrauterine device that cannot or will not be removed before initiation of study treatment. For nonmenopausal participants, at least one normal menstrual cycle is recommended after removal to confirm the absence of acute bleeding or infection before enrollment.
5. Histologically confirmed or highly suspected invasive cervical cancer, adenocarcinoma in situ, or high-grade glandular intraepithelial lesion.
6. Concomitant endocervical or vaginal lesions.
7. Any cervical treatment within 3 months before enrollment, including but not limited to LEEP, cold knife conization, ablation, cryotherapy, or other local physical or chemical treatment.
8. Known severe immunodeficiency, such as active AIDS or uncontrolled autoimmune disease, or ongoing systemic immunosuppressive therapy for 2 weeks or longer, chemotherapy, or other treatment considered by the investigator to substantially affect immune status or interfere with efficacy assessment.
9. Any contraindication to the use of the hexaminolevulinate cervical photodynamic therapy system, including:

invasive cervical cancer, adenocarcinoma in situ, cervical canal intraepithelial neoplasia, or other glandular epithelial lesions;
* a history of toxic shock syndrome;
* porphyria;
* known hypersensitivity to hexaminolevulinate or related aminolevulinate derivatives or any excipient;
* severe gynecologic infection identified by colposcopy or clinical examination;
* menstruation or active vaginal bleeding;
* known silicone allergy;
* presence of a cardiac pacemaker;
* cervical structural abnormality or incomplete cervical anatomy resulting from previous surgery that prevents appropriate use of the disposable cervical photodynamic therapy light device;
* pregnancy or delivery or abortion within the previous 6 weeks.
10. Any other condition that, in the investigator's medical judgment, may expose the participant to unacceptable risk or substantially interfere with study data collection or interpretation.

